# Profiling families and their bindings — the pack owners (migration 0069).
#
# WHAT A FAMILY IS. The unit of CONVERSATION, not of taxonomy. NCO has 3,449 occupations
# and unit group 7223 alone holds 44 near-identical machining titles; "Welder, Gas" and
# "Welder, Electric" differ by ~0.02 at occupation level and by NOTHING in what you would
# ask someone. So a family owns one question pack and claims a slice of the occupation
# tree through a binding, and the exact NCO code is settled later by the pack's own first
# question rather than by a disambiguation prompt listing 44 titles.
#
# SCOPE OF THIS FILE: the 41 highest-volume Indian blue-collar trades plus the mandatory
# universal fallback — deliberately NOT all ~200 families the plan targets. This matches
# the plan's own risk-register recommendation (#1: "cut a launch gate at ~40 families
# covering the top worker volumes"), and it is safe to stop here because the universal
# family keeps every unauthored trade working rather than dead. These 41 are exactly the
# trades the vernacular alias corpus already covers, so retrieval and packs stay in step.
#
# BINDINGS ARE AT ISCO UNIT LEVEL (specificity 40). Unit is the granularity where a shared
# question pack is honest: everyone in 7212 is welding something. Going finer would split
# families that ask identical questions; going coarser (minor/sub-major) would merge
# trades that do not — 72 covers welders AND toolmakers AND vehicle mechanics.
#
# canonical_role_id IS NULL ON MOST ROWS, and that is a finding rather than an omission.
# The match engine's role space is 13 ids, almost all CNC/welding/plumbing/carpentry
# (@badabhai/taxonomy ROLES), so it simply has no id for a tailor, a cook, a driver, a
# beautician or a waiter. Forcing a wrong crosswalk would poison ranking for those trades,
# so the column stays null until the role space is widened — a product decision, not one
# this file gets to make. Same for industry_id, where the entire vocabulary is two values.
#
# PRIVACY: occupation reference data and reviewed labels. No worker data.

# ── Families ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{"kind":"family","family_id":"fam_universal","label_en":"General","label_hi":"सामान्य"}
