# ── Batch 1, the desk trades: part programming and the drawing office ─────────
#
# THE REVIEW NOTE THAT HAS NOWHERE ELSE TO GO. `question_pack.review_note` is a column;
# `profiling_family` has none, so the three rows below carry their draft status HERE rather
# than in a JSON field the seeder would drop on the floor. THE LABELS, THE BINDINGS AND THE
# ALIAS TRANCHE THAT REACHES THEM ARE DRAFTED AND NOT YET RATIFIED — no practising part
# programmer or draughtsman has corrected this vocabulary. See
# docs/registers/trade-content-ratification.md, and the `review_note` on each of the three
# packs, which says the same thing where a column exists to say it.
#
# canonical_role_id IS SET ON TWO OF THE THREE, against this file's usual null. The header
# above explains why most rows leave it null — the match engine's role space simply has no id
# for a tailor or a cook. It has one here: `role_cam_programmer` (dom_programming) and
# `role_designer` (dom_design) are both live in @badabhai/taxonomy ROLES, so filling the column
# crosswalks these two trades into ranking instead of leaving them invisible to it.
#
# fam_draughting KEEPS BOTH COLUMNS NULL, and that is the same rule applied honestly rather
# than an omission. It is the unit-3118 router: it holds twelve occupations spanning civil,
# architectural, electrical, structural and topographical draughting plus the tracer and the
# blue printer. `role_designer` would over-claim eleven of them and `ind_industrial_
# manufacturing` is false for every civil one, so both stay null until the role space widens.
